-- D222: TWO EMITTING ARMS under `pair` — the first program in the language's
-- history that can OBSERVE the product's arm order.
--
-- Before D222 `t-pair-morph-check` fixed both arms to `pure`, so an effectful
-- arm was unwritable and the order was unobservable. `evalᴰ ⟨f,g⟩ a =
-- evalᴰ f a >>=T λ b → evalᴰ g a >>=T λ c → returnT (b , c)` specifies f FIRST,
-- and `obs-correct-pair` (D211) PROVES the machine matches — over arbitrary,
-- hence effectful, arms.
--
-- This is the same hazard D221 found one level up, where the cata's traversal
-- ran the mirror of what `seqF` specifies. The difference is that there the
-- correspondence was a POSTULATE and here it is a PROOF; this test is what makes
-- the proof falsifiable rather than merely believed.
--
-- Expected: exit code 7, trace [1, 2].

import I.Linux.Syscalls as S
import I.Test.Emit as E

both : Eff Int (Unit * Unit)
both = pair (compose emit@E (\_ -> 1)) (compose emit@E (\_ -> 2))

run : IO Unit
run = compose (\_ -> unit) (compose both (\_ -> 0))

main : IO Unit
main = compose exit@S (compose (\_ -> 7) run)
